    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "act": "PROBLEM",
      "role_note": "what schema drift actually is",
      "narration_text": "Method: schema drift is any unplanned change in a data source's structure — a field added, removed, renamed, or its meaning quietly shifted — that a downstream system wasn't built to expect. It's especially common wherever multiple systems or vendors feed into one pipeline, since nobody upstream is obligated to warn you before they change something.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "manim",
        "motion": "fade",
        "manim": {
          "scene_class": "B01_WhatItIs",
          "file": "scenes.py"
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 22,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 22.85,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim:B01_WhatItIs",
        "at": "2026-09-11T16:38:05"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "act": "OUTPUT",
      "role_note": "a real, documented case",
      "narration_text": "A real Azure Data Factory pipeline broke this way: a supplier added a field called Lot Batch Notes to their CSV. The ingestion job wasn't built to expect it, and downstream Power BI dashboards started showing nulls for key numbers — not because the data was wrong, but because the shape it arrived in had quietly changed.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "manim",
        "motion": "fade",
        "manim": {
          "scene_class": "B02_ARealCase",
          "file": "scenes.py"
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 22,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 21.01,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim:B02_ARealCase",
        "at": "2026-09-11T16:38:05"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "CHANGE",
      "role_note": "the same pattern at the field level",
      "narration_text": "Drift doesn't have to change the schema's shape to still break things. Sometimes a field stays exactly where it's supposed to be, but what counts as empty changes underneath you. One real system returned an empty string for a missing value. A check written to look for a missing value expected null instead. The field never changed position — what it meant when it was empty did.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "manim",
        "motion": "fade",
        "manim": {
          "scene_class": "B03_TheSamePattern",
          "file": "scenes.py"
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 22,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 22.59,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim:B03_TheSamePattern",
        "at": "2026-09-11T16:38:05"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "act": "OUTPUT",
      "role_note": "why this matters more as pipelines grow",
      "narration_text": "The more systems a piece of data passes through, the more chances there are for one of them to quietly redefine what a value means. A pipeline can run clean for months and still be carrying an assumption nobody's checked lately.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "manim",
        "motion": "fade",
        "manim": {
